  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A satellite recorded a nuclear explosion that no country detonated. In September 1979, American satellite Vela 6911 picked up a double flash off the African coast.",
        "visual_query": "1970s satellite orbit above ocean",
        "visual_fallback": "space satellite view"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Double flashes are the unmistakable signature of an atmospheric nuclear weapon test. The White House convened an emergency panel, while surveillance jets scrambled to sample the air.",
        "visual_query": "1970s military jet in clouds",
        "visual_fallback": "military jet sky"
      },
      {
        "narration": "While surveillance jets found no airborne particles, low-level fallout was later detected in Australian livestock, and naval hydrophones recorded acoustic signals consistent with an ocean surface blast.",
        "visual_query": "ocean hydrophone sensor underwater depth",
        "visual_fallback": "deep sea underwater"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The mystery deepened for decades until declassified documents revealed a hidden partnership. Nuclear intelligence now strongly points to a joint secret test conducted by South Africa and Israel.",
        "visual_query": "declassified government files research desk",
        "visual_fallback": "vintage spy documents"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Experts suggest the test involved a low-yield nuclear weapon, such as a neutron bomb or artillery round. Did two stealth allies successfully detonate an atomic weapon and hide it from the world?",
        "visual_query": "dark ocean water horizon sunrise",
        "visual_fallback": "dark ocean water"
      }
